WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.865
Human performance is the ability to do meaningful work reliably under real conditions.

00:00:04.865 --> 00:00:08.850
It is not the same as being busy, staying online all day, or pushing through every limit.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.885
It is the practical capacity to think clearly, act skillfully, communicate well,

00:00:12.885 --> 00:00:16.800
recover appropriately, and produce useful outcomes when the environment is imperfect.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.225
Performance is shaped by physical, cognitive, emotional, environmental,

00:00:20.225 --> 00:00:21.140
and social factors.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:25.910
Sleep, workload, stress, workspace setup, team communication, task clarity, tools, habits,

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.505
and recovery all influence how people actually work.

00:00:28.505 --> 00:00:32.465
A person may have strong motivation and still perform poorly if the system around them

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:34.000
creates constant interruption,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.110
unclear priorities, or unsustainable pace.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:38.400
Performance is also different from productivity.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.020
Productivity often focuses on output volume: how many tickets closed, words written,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.980
calls handled, or tasks completed.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:49.110
Performance asks a broader question: was the work correct, resilient, sustainable,

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.410
and appropriate to the risk?

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.150
In technical, operational, creative, and leadership roles,

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.385
quality and judgment matter as much as speed.

00:00:55.385 --> 00:00:59.270
The core idea of this course is that human performance is a system design problem,

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:00.800
not just a motivation problem.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.920
Unlimited focus, unlimited willpower, and constant peak output are myths.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.810
Strong performers build conditions that make good work repeatable: clear priorities,

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.710
protected attention, recovery, feedback, practice, decision supports,

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:14.915
and team norms that reduce avoidable friction.

00:01:14.915 --> 00:01:17.750
This systems view is useful because it creates more options.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.980
Instead of asking people to simply try harder,

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.160
leaders and individuals can adjust the workload, clarify the goal, change the environment,

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.955
add a checklist, improve handoffs, or create recovery time after intense work.

00:01:27.955 --> 00:01:32.560
Human performance improves when the design of work respects how people actually operate.
